      "docId": "dossier:engineering:9a8b7c6d5e4f:0",
      "sourceId": "9a8b7c6d5e4f",
      "text": "The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed on 7 November 1940, only four months after it opened, when wind of about forty miles per hour drove the deck into violent twisting oscillations.",
      "offset": 0
    },
    {
      "docId": "dossier:engineering:9a8b7c6d5e4f:1",
      "sourceId": "9a8b7c6d5e4f",
      "text": "Engineers later traced the failure to aeroelastic flutter, and the replacement bridge used a deeper, stiffer deck truss and open grating to let the wind pass through.",
      "offset": 185
    },
    {
      "docId": "dossier:engineering:123456abcdef:0",
      "sourceId": "123456abcdef",
      "text": "Concrete gains most of its strength by hydration, a chemical reaction between cement and water, and it continues to harden for years after it is poured.",
      "offset": 0
    }
  ],
  "missing": []
